NNAAMMEE
     ffssiirraanndd — randomize inode generation numbers

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ffssiirraanndd [--bb] [--ff] [--pp] _s_p_e_c_i_a_l [_s_p_e_c_i_a_l _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffssiirraanndd utility installs random generation numbers on all the inodes
     for each file system specified on the command line by _s_p_e_c_i_a_l.  This in‐
     creases the security of NFS-exported file systems by making it difficult
     to ``guess'' filehandles.

     _N_o_t_e: newfs(8) now does the equivalent of ffssiirraanndd itself so it is no
     longer necessary to run ffssiirraanndd by hand on a new file system.  It is only
     used to re-randomize or report on an existing file system.

     The ffssiirraanndd utility should only be used on an unmounted file system that
     has been checked with fsck(8) or a file system that is mounted read-only.
     The ffssiirraanndd utility may be used on the root file system in single-user
     mode but the system should be rebooted via ``reboot -n'' afterwards.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The available options are as follows:

     --bb      Use the default block size (usually 512 bytes) instead of the
             value gleaned from the disklabel.

     --ff      Force ffssiirraanndd to run even if the file system on _s_p_e_c_i_a_l is not
             marked as clean.

     --pp      Print the current generation numbers for all inodes instead of
             generating new ones.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     fs(5), fsck(8), newfs(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ffssiirraanndd utility appeared in SunOS 3.x.

     This version of ffssiirraanndd first appeared in OpenBSD 2.1.

     A FreeBSD version first appeared in FreeBSD 2.2.5.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
